724	 ## The sea
725	 
726	-- **Map format and pipeline.** The map is `assets/source/maps/slice-sea.tmj`, in a documented subset of Tiled's JSON format (`packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`): orthogonal, finite, 24 px tiles (`TILE_PIXELS`, as drawn); one imageless tileset whose tiles carry a `type` of `sea`, `land`, `shoal` or `dock`; a `terrain` layer as a plain gid array; an `objects` layer with a named `island` rectangle per island (property `theme`), a `spawn` point per slot (properties `slot` and `facing`) and one `hub` point. Nothing else is accepted. `assets/source/maps/sketch.ts` writes it from one mirrored ASCII quarter. `npm run map:build` (`scripts/build-map.ts`) converts it into `packages/content/src/maps/sliceSea.ts` (terrain rows and objects) if it passes the validators; a unit test fails when that module is stale (it reads the `.tmj` as a Vite `?raw` import).
727	-- **Validators.** `validateSeaMap` checks §2 and §3: four small islands, each land tile inside one island rectangle, each island with a dock beside its land and open sea; shoals; eight spawn slots on open sea outside the islands' bounding box and pairwise beyond `ENGAGEMENT_RANGE_TILES` (6); every dock and slot reachable by sea; and, by eight-way shortest paths (no cut corners) at full speed: each island's nearest neighbour 20–40 s away, no dock-or-slot crossing over 90 s, a dock within 40 s of every slot. No empty stretches: every navigable tile is within the sight radius of land, a shoal or a dock (not the edge). Routes meet: closing the disc around the hub (open sea near the centre; radius half the sight radius, so ships in it see each other) lengthens or cuts every shortest path between docks of different islands. The slice sea (111 × 111 tiles, midline shoal walls open only in the hub's disc): neighbours 26 s apart, longest crossing 39 s, water within 9.9 tiles of an obstacle, 91 % open sea.
728	+- **Map format and pipeline.** The map is `assets/source/maps/slice-sea.tmj`, in a documented subset of Tiled's JSON format (`packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`): orthogonal, finite, 24 px tiles (`TILE_PIXELS`, as drawn); one imageless tileset whose tiles carry a `type` of `sea`, `land`, `shoal` or `dock`; a `terrain` layer as a plain gid array; an `objects` layer with a named `island` rectangle per island (property `theme`), a `spawn` point per slot (properties `slot` and `facing`) and one `hub` point. Nothing else is accepted. `assets/source/maps/sketch.ts` writes it from one mirrored ASCII quarter (`sliceSeaSketch.ts`; a unit test fails when they differ). `npm run map:build` (`scripts/build-map.ts`) converts it into `packages/content/src/maps/sliceSea.ts` (terrain rows and objects) if it passes the validators; a unit test fails when that module is stale (it reads the `.tmj` as a Vite `?raw` import).
729	+- **Validators.** `validateSeaMap` checks §2 and §3: four small islands, each land tile inside one island rectangle, each island with a dock beside its land and open sea; shoals; eight spawn slots on open sea outside the islands' bounding box and pairwise beyond `ENGAGEMENT_RANGE_TILES` (6); every dock and slot reachable by sea; and, by eight-way shortest paths (no cut corners) at full speed: each island's nearest neighbour 20–40 s away, no dock-or-slot crossing over 90 s, a dock within 40 s of every slot. No empty stretches: every navigable tile is within the sight radius of land, a shoal or a dock (not the edge). Routes meet: closing the disc around the hub (open sea near the centre; radius half the sight radius, so ships in it see each other) lengthens or cuts every shortest path between docks of different islands. At most 112 tiles a side. The slice sea (111 × 111 tiles, midline shoal walls open only in the hub's disc): neighbours 26 s apart, longest crossing 39 s, water within 9.9 tiles of an obstacle, 91 % open sea.
730	 - **Sailing.** Positions are integer sub-units (256 a tile); headings are 64 directions, 0 east, clockwise (y grows down), unit vectors from a literal cosine table. Each world step the throttle (`-moveY`) accelerates towards 39 sub-units a step (3.05 tiles a second), brakes, or reverses slowly from a stop; `moveX` turns a heading a step (3.2 s a circle); none coasts to a stop. On 24 px tiles that handles like the old five tiles a second on 16 px (unit tests hold it within 25 %). The hull is a square of half-size 80. A move sweeps each axis in turn through the tile columns or rows its leading edge enters and stops flush at the first land, shoal or map edge: a ship slides along an obstacle met at an angle, runs aground head-on, and never tunnels.
731	-- **Docking.** Interact (E) at speed 15 or less, whatever the throttle (a ship driven ashore at its dock docks once stopped), with the ship's centre within 1.5 tiles of a dock tile's centre and a clear hull along the straight run to it (checked sub-unit by sub-unit) moors it: it moves to the dock's centre, stops, and records the dock; turning does nothing. It stays moored while E or the throttle it moored with (`mooredThrottle`) is held; pressing a throttle after releasing that one, or the other direction, casts off at once. The page's keyboard keeps a tap until its next read, the server a button until its next step, so no quick tap of E is lost; the HUD's Harbour line says "Press E to dock" or "Slow down to dock" by the same rule.
732	+- **Docking.** Hold E near a dock to brake and moor, at any speed and throttle: with the ship's centre within 1.5 tiles of a dock tile's centre and a clear hull along the straight run to it (checked sub-unit by sub-unit), it moves to the dock's centre, stops and records the dock, so an angled arrival moors, not slides past. It stays moored while E or the throttle it moored with (`mooredThrottle`) is held; pressing a throttle after releasing that one, or the other direction, casts off. An input stall does not: the server gives a lapsed client no input, keeping the latch. The keyboard keeps a tap of E until its next read (not past a blur), the server until its next step; within reach the HUD says "Press E to dock".
733	 - **Visibility boundary (§16).** The room's `ships` map is tagged `.view()`. Each client gets a `StateView`; after every mirror the room puts into it the client's own ship and each ship within `SIGHT_RADIUS_TILES` (10, `visibleShipIds`) and takes out the rest, so Colyseus encodes no other ship into that client's patches (an integration test checks the decoded state and the raw frames). `players` in the state is the public player count. Terrain is static content every client bundles, never synced; the minimap fog is client-side presentation, not a security boundary.
734	 - **Test placement.** Tests place ships through `POST /__test/place` (code, session id, tile, heading); its tile bounds come from the map. Only the dev and test entry (`apps/server/src/dev.ts`) offers it, when `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_PLACEMENT=1`; the production entry (`main.ts`) never reads that, which the build smoke test checks. It only moves a ship: admission and views are unchanged.
